    "chunk_id": "2026-07-02-islandlk-07713303-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8972508481c69ededec4fa968ed81e81",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1928,
    "chunk_text": "BYD building Sri Lanka\u2019s EV ecosystem beyond the showroom\n\nThe electric vehicle revolution is entering a new phase in Sri Lanka \u2014 where the battle is no longer only about selling vehicles, but about building the confidence, technology and service ecosystem that will define the future of mobility.\n\nAs the country embraces new energy vehicles (NEVs), BYD, the world\u2019s largest manufacturer of NEVs, is strengthening its long-term commitment to Sri Lanka through a major expansion of aftersales infrastructure, technical capability and customer support.\n\nWithin just nine months of vehicle imports resuming, BYD has secured a commanding position in Sri Lanka\u2019s automotive market, accounting for 31.1% of all brand-new passenger vehicle registrations and 63.3% of new electric vehicle registrations, according to company data.\n\nBut industry leaders emphasise that the real test of EV adoption lies beyond the initial purchase.\n\n\u201cThe future of electric mobility will not be decided only by the vehicle on the road. It will depend on the confidence customers have in the technology, servicing capability and long-term support, a senior official said.\n\nHowever, electric vehicles introduce a completely new technology landscape involving high-voltage battery systems, advanced software, electronic controls and specialised diagnostics.\n\nBYD Sri Lanka, represented through authorised distributor John Keells CG Auto, has focused on developing a dedicated NEV aftersales ecosystem to address these challenges.\n\n\u201cAn EV is not simply another vehicle with a different engine. It is a technology platform on wheels. Therefore, servicing requires specialised knowledge, equipment and continuous training, an industry expert said.\n\nBYD\u2019s aftersales network has already completed more than 25,000 service and repair operations, while its service centres have the capacity to handle more than 150 vehicles daily across the island."
  },
