    "chunk_size_bytes": 1939,
    "chunk_text": "Lower tea offerings spur stronger bidding at Colombo auction\n\nThe auction offerings declined to 6.0 million kilogrammes (M/Kgs), from 6.2 M/Kgs on offer the previous week.\n\nThere was improved activity, perhaps due to a combination of the declining auction offerings in the forthcoming sales and a gradual improvement in product quality.\n\nThe Ex-Estate offerings were fairly similar to last and totalled 0.94 M/Kgs. The overall quality was mostly similar to last.\n\nSelectively, the teas from the Western slopes appreciated by Rs.20-40 per kilogramme. The Nuwara Eliyas were somewhat a disappointing feature, with limited activity.\n\nThe Uda Pussellawas witnessed some recovery, consequent to improved activity at the lower end of the market.\n\nThe Uvas, here again, the teas in the higher price bracket, were easier, whilst the improved activity at the lower end resulted in the bottom end of the market gaining by Rs.20-40 per kilogramme.\n\nThe High and Medium Grown CTC teas were more sellable around the previous week\u2019s levels, whilst the corresponding Low Grown types sold well at firm to dearer rates. Limited activity was seen from the shippers to the UK, continent and South Africa. Selective buying was seen from Japan and China, whilst the shippers to the Middle East and CIS bid on a wider cross-section of teas.\n\nThe Low Growns totalled approximately 2.4 M/Kgs, with all categories witnessing a fair demand.\n\nIn the Leafy and Semi-Leafy catalogues, the high-priced BOP1s were easier, whilst the balance sold around last levels.\n\nThe OP1s, in general, were firm to dearer. The well-made OPs appreciated, whilst the balance together with the OPAs was firm.\n\nThe high-priced PEKs together with the Best and Below Best PEK1s appreciated, whilst the balance sold at last levels.\n\nIn the Tippy catalogue, the FBOPs in general appreciated. The Select Best FF1s gained, whilst the balance in general was firm to dearer, following quality."
