    "md5": "e4960312d6b6e0b7637a88230582aeb0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1558,
    "chunk_text": "A new life in Saudi Arabia and Romance en route in Sri Lanka\n\nA telegram brought the news that I\u2019d been hoping for: Dr. Reda Al-Thiga, Dean of the Graduate School at Dhahran University, would be in Boston and wished to interview me. All my travel and accommodation costs for the interview would be reimbursed. On the scheduled day, I left Pittsburgh and checked into a luxurious five-star hotel which was also the interview venue. The meeting was set for six in the evening so I took a shower, got ready and went to the room I was summoned to.\n\nWhen I entered the room, Dean Al-Thiga was watching the English news (CNN) on television. He kindly offered me a seat for me and asked me to wait until the newscast was over. So, I sat beside him, watching the news.\n\nA week later after, another telegram arrived\u2014I had been selected for the position of Associate Professor of Mechanical Engineering at the university in Saudi Arabia. The message included details of my salary and benefits. I was asked to come to Dhahran that August to assume duties. In response, I sent a telegram expressing my gratitude but also saying that the offered salary was not sufficient.\n\nAfter some discussion on the telephone, I was told that higher salaries were paid to American citizens and I did not qualify. I responded that I was a green card holder (permanent resident of the US) when I applied for the job and had since become a naturalized citizen of the United States. On hearing this, they offered me a much higher salary and the matter was resolved to my satisfaction."
  },
