    "chunk_text": "After some discussion on the telephone, I was told that higher salaries were paid to American citizens and I did not qualify. I responded that I was a green card holder (permanent resident of the US) when I applied for the job and had since become a naturalized citizen of the United States. On hearing this, they offered me a much higher salary and the matter was resolved to my satisfaction.\n\nAs planned, after the conference in San Francisco I flew to Sri Lanka for a holiday before going on to Saudi Arabia to take my faculty position there. Unexpected romance started during that visit. Was it a rebound, because I had left Audrey\u2014with whom I shared a loving relationship in Pittsburgh? I couldn\u2019t say.\n\nIn Sri Lanka, my two brothers came to meet me in Colombo. After dinner, my brothers suggested we go to the home of the sister of their friend Hema. He was a science graduate from University of Ceylon, Colombo, and was a teacher in a government school in the capital. When we arrived there, we were told that Hema had gone to the home of a friend (also a friend of my oldest brother), to tutor the children in that household. So we headed for that house.\n\nHema was not there but the house-owner, who was a widow, had just returned from England where her husband who was ill with a liver disease had passed away. Her mother greeted us and insisted we had a cup of tea before leaving. The mother went inside the house and I heard her telling the daughter about us and the lady came out to greet us. My oldest brother had known her. I remember her wearing a black skirt and a cream color blouse and looking depressed.\n\nAfter listening to her at length, I advised her to go for physiotherapy\u2014a term she later admitted she didn\u2019t know. I felt sorry for her because she had three young children (a girl and two boys) and seemed helpless. Even though she appeared older than I, she was pretty, pleasant and kind."
  },
