    "chunk_size_bytes": 1734,
    "chunk_text": "The university land was vast, just as I had read in the manual. There were trees newly planted in fresh soil, tall date palms, and massive concrete buildings. There were also neatly arranged and modern staff quarters and a well-planned road network. On one side, I could see the Arabian Sea, part of the mighty Indian Ocean. The scorching heat could not be eased even by the sight of water. The buildings and vehicles were air-conditioned, which was a blessing. Whenever a slight breeze passed, fine sand would rise and enter my nostrils, mouth, ears, and eyes.\n\nThe house provided to me was spacious\u2014large enough for a family\u2014and fully air-conditioned. It had everything that a home would need\u2014furniture, refrigerator, electric stove, pots, pans, wooden cupboards, crockery, cutlery, storage boxes, mats, and even linen (bed sheets, pillowcases, towels). All I needed to buy was a vehicle, a television set, and a music system.\n\nThere were a few small shops near the university where I could buy groceries. A bus service was available to travel to the city. In the bustling marketplace known as the souq or souk in Dhahran, one could buy vehicles, electronic appliances, clothes, and other essentials. Since there were no taxes, and because Asian workers were paid low wages, along with the abundance of cheap fuel, goods were remarkably inexpensive. There were similar marketplaces in other cities including the nearby Al-Khobar.\n\nThe university had workers from India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, and Sri Lanka. Many of them were Muslims, and they preferred traditional Arab attire\u2014robes and head coverings\u2014in place of the pants and shirts they wore at home. This they did, I was told, in order to curry favour with the Saudis."
