    "chunk_text": "In the case of educationists the issue is further complicated by the fact that almost all appearing for the interview possessed post-graduate qualifications; some of them had written books; others had written numerous articles; some had won awards and prizes and so on. Everyone brings all these to show the interview board. Some are so heavily laden that they stagger in. Others have had to resort to the assistance of some of the office aides. Some of them bring in so much material that these have to be accommodated on two or three chairs in large heaps.\n\nI have seen too many interview boards where the principal consideration seemed to be the time constraints and the convenience of the members of the board. Under my watch, I was not going to let this happen in education. We therefore set a number of days, including Saturdays and finally finished a few days before Christmas. I felt a sense of great relief and satisfaction only marred by the sudden death of a lady principal who had retired and who had been the principal of a leading school in the island. She had fared exceptionally well at the interview. She was knocked down by a vehicle, and died without enjoying the satisfaction that comes from the due recognition of her services, and a little enhancement of her pension.\n\nLess than two weeks to go in the public service and induction into the private sector\n\nBy now I had less than two weeks to go in service. Strangely enough, I was not feeling any sense of loss or regret but one of relief. The government had not made any offer of any firm assignment with the exception of two telephone calls from the Foreign Secretary, Mr. Wilhelm Woutersz, which was more personal to the effect that I should now give my mind to going abroad as an Ambassador. Neither my wife nor I was interested in living in a foreign country for a number of years. What I was looking for was some assignment or assignments that did not confine me within four walls for eight hours."
