    "chunk_size_bytes": 1554,
    "chunk_text": "The major difference between working at senior levels in the private sector, as opposed to the public sector is that in the public sector everybody else manages your time. You don\u2019t. In the private sector you manage your time. Nobody else does. In the public sector, the disturbance factor is consistently very high, and often the merely urgent overtakes and overwhelms the important mostly in situations where the urgent is relatively trivial.\n\nThen there are the interminable meetings and conferences where much time is wasted because no system had been developed where those who had perhaps one item in a long agenda, were dealt with and sent away. One wasted so much time just waiting. The great English poet John Milton wrote in a famous poem, \u201cThey also serve who only stand and wait.\u201d In our context, they also serve who only sit and wait. Meetings and conferences came so thick and fast that on one notable occasion I had invitations to be present at a meeting with the President, the Prime Minister, and Parliament on the same day at the same time! I chose according to the Precedence Table.\n\nIn the private sector you are free of all these. They are interested in results, not in processes or polemics. Nobody disturbs you or tells you what to do. You could always talk to people if you want to. But that is up to you. Otherwise you just do what you have to do. Nobody is really bothered as to whether you did your work in an office, in the car, at home, or over lunch or dinner at a hotel or at the sea beach. What matters is the result."
