    "chunk_size_bytes": 1623,
    "chunk_text": "Rather than accepting the situation as inevitable, they analysed the problem systematically. They measured the frequency of errors, identified which departments generated the problematic forms and categorised the types of handwriting mistakes that caused the greatest difficulties. Their investigations eventually led them to develop a standard manual prescribing how numerals and letters should be written on official forms. Management accepted the proposal and implemented it throughout the organisation. The result was a dramatic reduction in data-entry errors.\n\nThe lesson was unmistakable. Quality Circles were not confined to factories. They could improve any type of work where people were encouraged to think, analyse and solve problems together.\n\nOn my journey home, my Chairman requested that I stop over in the Philippines to visit the BF Goodrich tyre factory. At that time, the Sri Lanka Tyre Corporation had a technical collaboration agreement with BF Goodrich of the United States, making the visit particularly worthwhile.\n\nThe Philippine factory employed around 1,000 workers and produced approximately 2,000 tyres per day. By contrast, our own factory employed nearly 2,000 workers but produced only about 1,000 tyres. The figures spoke volumes about our productivity\u2014or rather, our lack of it.\n\nThe factory also operated an employee suggestion scheme, but there was a significant difference. It was restricted to engineers and managerial staff, and each participant was expected to generate perhaps one worthwhile suggestion each year. Many of these suggestions produced considerable financial savings."
