    "md5": "5660969addf1edfae8e6c229b4a47056",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1852,
    "chunk_text": "The factory also operated an employee suggestion scheme, but there was a significant difference. It was restricted to engineers and managerial staff, and each participant was expected to generate perhaps one worthwhile suggestion each year. Many of these suggestions produced considerable financial savings.\n\nIt was during this period that I began to appreciate the profound philosophical difference between Western and Japanese management.\n\nThe Japanese themselves often expressed it rather elegantly. They observed that traditional American management made use primarily of the \u201chands\u201d of employees. Japanese management, on the other hand, sought to harness not only the hands but also the minds of every employee.\n\nThat simple philosophy explained much of Japan\u2019s remarkable industrial success.\n\nFilled with enthusiasm, I began introducing the concept on my return to Sri Lanka. Unfortunately, enthusiasm alone was not enough.\n\nMany Heads of Divisions saw little reason to change established practices. From their perspective, everything appeared to be functioning satisfactorily. Why introduce a completely new management concept that encouraged workers to participate in decision-making?\n\nSeveral senior colleagues quietly advised me to proceed with caution. They explained that our Chairman\u2019s management style was generally regarded as autocratic and that worker participation initiatives might not be welcomed. Although they appreciated my enthusiasm, they feared I was moving too quickly.\n\nRather than abandoning the idea, I decided to persevere more discreetly. Experience has taught me that genuine organisational change often requires patience, persistence and careful timing. New ideas seldom encounter universal acceptance at the outset. Those proposing change must be prepared for disappointment before success eventually arrives."
  },
