    "md5": "88b4598e608ee5841925cb86060ac47b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1693,
    "chunk_text": "Rather than abandoning the idea, I decided to persevere more discreetly. Experience has taught me that genuine organisational change often requires patience, persistence and careful timing. New ideas seldom encounter universal acceptance at the outset. Those proposing change must be prepared for disappointment before success eventually arrives.\n\nOne day I received an unexpected parcel from the BF Goodrich head office.\n\nInside were numerous articles documenting both successful and unsuccessful Quality Circle implementations across Europe. The collection proved immensely valuable. It demonstrated that Quality Circles were by no means guaranteed to succeed. Many organisations had failed because they treated the concept as another management programme rather than as a long-term cultural transformation requiring patience, leadership and trust.\n\nLooking back, I now realise how fortunate I was to receive them. They taught me as much from the failures as from the successes.\n\nInterestingly, 1980 was also the year when worldwide enthusiasm for Quality Circles reached its peak. Across Europe, North America and Asia, organisations were experimenting with this revolutionary Japanese approach to employee participation. Many embraced it enthusiastically, while others misunderstood its underlying philosophy and abandoned it prematurely.\n\nAbout a year after my return from Japan, I accepted a more senior appointment at another state-owned enterprise. Although considerably smaller than the Tyre Corporation, the organisation gave me much greater authority and freedom to introduce new ideas.\n\nI immediately recognised an opportunity to put into practice everything I had learnt in Japan."
